+[#virtual-capstone]
+[appendix]
+= Draft: Trusted-OS Virtual Capstone Profile
+
+== Status and scope
+
+This appendix describes the experimental *supervised virtual C-mode* interface
+implemented in
+https://github.com/project-starch/capstone-qemu/tree/6bf04634a2c28e0cf96488ac805287b89027fa02[QEMU revision 6bf04634a2c2].
+The corresponding Linux module, runtime and application baseline is
+https://github.com/project-starch/llvm-capstone/tree/6bf819677207292af1c26df87b5fd6200b1afe93[llvm-capstone revision 6bf819677207].
+The base academic reference at this review is revision `ac7f32910048`.
+
+This is an *implementation profile for review*, including experimental opcode
+allocations and platform ABI details. It neither freezes a portable ISA nor
+claims an RTL implementation. Where this appendix differs from the earlier
+chapters, it describes only the trusted-OS profile. The existing physical
+domain interface remains a separate profile.
+
+The kernel, firmware and privileged adapter are trusted. Linux supplies page
+tables, physical backing and scheduling. The application executes Capstone
+instructions using virtual addresses; it does not acquire kernel authority.
+The qualified implementation uses one hart, Sv39, pinned private anonymous
+backing and no cross-process sharing of tagged pages. Multiple threads of a
+process share its address space and lifetime namespace.
+
+The earlier capability-enabled U-mode experiment is distinct. In particular,
+its Linux entry patch, `cepc` return protocol and proposed `CSCHECKR/W`
+instructions are not requirements of this supervised C-mode interface.
+
+== Ownership and addressing
+
+An address space A has page-table root P_A and lifetime namespace N_A.
+A capability's address bounds and cursor denote virtual addresses in A;
+its lifetime identity is logically `(N_A, local_node_id)`.
+The namespace is selected by the running context, not encoded in each
+capability. Equal node numbers or equal virtual ranges in different address
+spaces confer no shared authority.
+
+For a data access, the capability tag, type, rights, complete bounds and
+node liveness must authorize the access. The virtual address is then
+translated using P_A with *user* PTE permissions. Page permissions can
+restrict capability authority, never enlarge it. Tags attach to aligned
+16-byte *physical* granules, so aliases see the same tag. An ordinary write
+clears tags of overlapping granules, including writes through kernel aliases.
+
+The trusted adapter must control minting, mappings and namespace selection:
+it must not create overlapping live linear authority through independently
+minted roots or aliases, copy a tagged value into another namespace, or reuse
+a table/frame while saved authority still names it. Current private mapping
+restrictions make those obligations manageable. Virtual addressing alone
+does not enforce the adapter's allocation policy.
+
+The existing linear transfer rules apply within N_A. A move transfers
+authority; it does not duplicate it. Non-linear capabilities may be copied.
+Rust language ownership, allocator object boundaries and this architectural
+model are separate layers: temporal object protection also requires the
+allocator to create and revoke the appropriate object lifetimes.
+
+=== Revocation
+
+N_A is abstractly a forest. A node is live if its ID denotes an allocated,
+valid record in N_A. ID zero, reserved IDs, out-of-range IDs, free records and
+missing or invalid tables are not live.
+
+`REVOKE R` invalidates the proper descendants of `node(R)` in N_A.
+Its existing operand type rule and LIN/UNINIT result rule are retained.
+The operand node is not itself retired by `REVOKE`.
+This replaces global bounds-overlap aliasing as the revocation domain for
+this profile; matching ranges in another address space are unaffected.
+`CSRETIRE` additionally invalidates a trusted arena ancestor itself.
+
+The implementation uses a depth-ordered doubly linked list. `MREV` inserts a
+senior node and deepens its operand; `SPLIT` inserts a sibling at the same
+depth. A revocation walks the subsequent deeper run, validates it before
+mutation, invalidates it and unlinks the run. Ancestor validity is not checked
+by walking parent pointers at every access: merely clearing an ancestor's
+valid bit would therefore be insufficient. The existing model exercises the
+actual C list algorithm and guest-table backend.
+
+== Execution and entry
+
+`CSRUNV` enters a saved virtual context from trusted S mode or scalar M mode.
+The implementation internally uses the existing C-mode privilege encoding
+(M with capability memory enabled), together with a virtual-context flag.
+It overrides address translation to use user PTE permissions and restricts
+privileged operations. This is not unrestricted architectural M-mode access
+for application code.
+
+PCC is checked before instruction bytes are read for translation and again
+when the translated instruction executes. The check covers liveness, type
+(LIN or NONLIN), X permission and the whole instruction within bounds, with
+overflow-safe arithmetic. Cached translated code must fail after revocation.
+The present emulator uses one instruction per virtual translation block.
+
+Ordinary capability manipulation, loads/stores and capability jumps remain
+available. The virtual instruction policy rejects `SEAL`, `CALL`, `RETURN`,
+`CAPENTER`, `CCSRRW`, supervisor entries and debug instructions. Privileged
+SYSTEM operations other than `ECALL` and `EBREAK` are rejected. CSR accesses
+requiring supervisor privilege and the legacy Capstone CSRs `0x800..0x803`
+are rejected; implemented user CSRs remain subject to normal CSR checks.
+An illegal operation raises cause 2.
+
+The adapter services an application `ECALL` after `CSRUNV` returns an event.
+Service numbers, buffer validation, Linux syscalls and pthread operations
+belong to the software ABI, not to new processor opcodes. No direct
+application-to-Linux syscall fast path is specified here.
+
+=== Trusted S-mode transfers
+
+The opt-in also permits the existing `LDC` and `STC` encodings in S mode,
+using a scalar kernel virtual address for the slot and preserving full tagged
+register values. These use the consuming protected-transfer rules below;
+ordinary S-mode loads remain scalar. This support is needed by the current
+module as well as by the separate Linux entry experiment.
+
+The module briefly uses tagged temporary registers to load, convert with
+`DELIN`/`SCC`, and store freshly minted grants. It excludes interrupts and
+preemption during that sequence and clears the temporary before returning
+to ordinary kernel code. Therefore the interface must not be described as
+"the kernel never holds tagged registers." It avoids a kernel-wide tagged
+context-switch ABI by containing these operations within the trusted adapter.
+
+== Added instruction encodings
+
+All three instructions use the R-type layout: `funct7[31:25]`, `rs2[24:20]`,
+`rs1[19:15]`, `funct3[14:12]`, `rd[11:7]`, `opcode[6:0]`.
+The experimental QEMU profile must be enabled (`x-capstone-u-mode=on`).
+
+[%header,cols="1,1,1,1,3"]
+|===
+|Mnemonic |Opcode |funct3 |funct7 |Operands
+|`CSRUNV` |`0x5b` |`1` |`0x24` |`rd`, physical frame address in `rs1`, action in `rs2`
+|`CSMINT` |`0x5b` |`1` |`0x50` |`rd`, destination slot VA in `rs1`, descriptor VA in `rs2`
+|`CSRETIRE` |`0x5b` |`1` |`0x53` |`rd`, scalar ancestor ID in `rs1`, use zero for `rs2`
+|===
+
+`CSRUNV` and `CSRETIRE` are trusted S/scalar-M operations; they reject U mode
+and physical or virtual C mode. `CSMINT` rejects U mode and is also denied by
+the virtual C instruction policy. Its current decoder still accepts legacy
+physical C mode; it is not a general application mint instruction.
+The existing physical supervisor opcodes `0x22` and `0x23` predate this profile.
+There is no implemented `CSCHECKR` or `CSCHECKW` at the reference revision.
+
+=== CSMINT
+
+The descriptor consists of three little-endian 64-bit words: `base`,
+exclusive `end`, and `perms` (X=1, W=2, R=4). It is 8-byte aligned.
+Each word is translated separately in the caller's address space.
+The destination is a 16-byte-aligned writable ordinary-RAM slot, translated
+with the caller's current permissions. All descriptor, slot, representability
+and table-capacity checks precede changes to records, destination bytes or tags.
+
+`base < end`, `perms <= 7`, and exact compression/decompression of the
+requested bounds and initial cursor `base` are required. Failure of these
+value checks raises cause 29 with zero fault data. Alignment and translation
+failures use the corresponding ordinary load/store exceptions.
+Two free node IDs are required; exhaustion raises cause 30 with zero data.
+
+Success creates a private senior ancestor and a linear child, stores a tagged
+child capability in the slot and returns the ancestor ID in scalar `rd`.
+The trusted adapter retains that ID for arena retirement. Discarding `rd`
+does not suppress allocation. Application derivations remain below the
+private ancestor, including subsequent application `MREV` operations.
+
+=== CSRETIRE
+
+The selected guest table is `srevroot`. The operation validates and walks the
+ancestor's proper descendants, invalidates and unlinks them, then invalidates
+and unlinks the ancestor. It returns scalar zero on success or one for an
+invalid identity/table or rejected list walk. `rs2` is currently ignored.
+Retirement does not itself unmap pages, clear every stale tag, or free IDs.
+The trusted adapter retires authority before mapping/backing reuse and uses
+the collection protocol before recycling node identities.
+
+== Control registers
+
+[%header,cols="1,1,1,4"]
+|===
+|CSR |Address |Access |Meaning
+|`scapctl` |`0x5c0` |S/M read/write
+|Bit 0 selects the separate protected-U experiment; other bits read as zero.
+It is not the virtual C entry mechanism.
+|`srevroot` |`0x5c1` |S/M read/write
+|Physical guest-table base; low 12 bits are cleared on write. Zero selects no table.
+|`urevavail` |`0xcc0` |U/S/M read-only
+|Free IDs in the selected valid guest table; zero for an invalid or missing table.
+|===
+
+The virtual application may read `urevavail` but cannot write the roots.
+Virtual contexts use their bound guest table; protected U uses the selected
+table when enabled; S uses it when `srevroot` is nonzero. Legacy physical
+C/M execution retains its host-tree backend. Privileged lifetime operations
+explicitly target the guest table. A namespace switch must change both
+translation and lifetime state consistently; QEMU restores them on context
+entry/escape and flushes translation state when required.
+
+== Guest lifetime table ABI
+
+The table starts at a 4-KiB-aligned physical RAM address and consists of
+16-byte slots. Its complete range must be ordinary writable RAM. It is
+kernel-owned, inaccessible for application modification and physically
+contiguous in this implementation. Process-local node IDs do not imply a
+pageable node table. All multibyte fields are little-endian.
+
+[%header,cols="1,1,4"]
+|===
+|Offset |Width |Field
+|`0` |64 bits |Capacity, including reserved slots; bit 63 enables recycling
+|`8` |64 bits |`next`, the high-water mark; initially 1, or 2 with recycling
+|`16 * id + 0` |32 bits |Previous ID; zero terminates a list
+|`16 * id + 4` |32 bits |Next ID; zero terminates a list
+|`16 * id + 8` |32 bits |Depth
+|`16 * id + 12` |32 bits |Flags: VALID=1, LINEAR=2, FREE=4
+|===
+
+Capacity is at most `2^31`; `next` must lie between the first usable ID and
+capacity. IDs must be below `next`, not merely below capacity.
+Slot zero is always the header. In recycling mode slot one is also reserved:
+offsets 16 and 20 contain 32-bit free-list head and count, and offset 24
+contains the 64-bit allocation counter. Its first usable ID is two.
+`urevavail = capacity - next + free_count` for a valid table (free count is
+zero without recycling). Monotone mode never reuses an issued ID.
+
+Record updates use the same list algorithm for host and guest stores.
+Operations preflight the needed records and capacity before mutation;
+revocation has a visit bound. These checks assume a trusted table owner and
+the prototype's stable RAM map, not concurrent modification by another hart
+or a malicious OS.
+
+== CSRUNV context ABI
+
+The caller owns a pinned, 16-byte-aligned physical frame in writable ordinary
+RAM, not application-accessible. A frame address identifies a saved context
+on the hart. There are currently 32 supervisor slots, shared with the
+physical supervisor; this is an implementation limit, not a process quota.
+
+[%header,cols="1,1,4"]
+|===
+|Offset |Bytes |Meaning
+|0 |8 |Initial `satp`; mode must be Sv39 (8)
+|8 |8 |Initial nonzero guest-table root
+|16 |8 |Output event kind
+|24 |8 |Output cause
+|32 |8 |Output event PC
+|40 |8 |Output fault address/data
+|48 |8 |Legacy raw low-word a0 result; use the cursor views below for capabilities
+|56 |8 |Options: bit 0 resumable ECALL; bit 1 resumable page faults
+|64 |16 |Initial tagged PCC
+|`64 + 16*i`, i=1..31 |16 each |Initial full-width x1..x31, including physical tags
+|576..639 |8 each |Output scalar cursor/value views of a0..a7 when either option is set
+|640 |8 |Collection page count
+|648 |8 |Physical address of the collection page-address array
+|===
+
+The base frame is 576 bytes. Nonzero options require 640 bytes; collection
+requires 656 bytes. Unknown option bits are rejected. Initial admission checks
+require a tagged PCC; the execution check enforces its type, rights, bounds
+and liveness. Root/header validity is enforced by table accesses.
+
+On first entry the processor transfers PCC and GPR slots into the context and
+clears all 32 source slots, including bytes and tags. Platform capability
+registers start empty. Floating-point and vector state, when present, are
+initialized and preserved with the context. Subsequent resumptions use the
+saved state, not new register values written into the input frame. Roots and
+options remain bound even if the caller rewrites that frame.
+
+[%header,cols="1,2,4"]
+|===
+|Action |Name |Behavior
+|0 |Enter/resume |Create a context or resume a paused non-service event at its saved PC
+|1 |Forget |Discard its saved supervisor state; return zero, including if no slot exists
+|2 |Service reply |For a paused ECALL, transfer frame slot 224 to a0, clear that slot, and resume at PC+4
+|3 |Collect |Sweep the paused context's namespace without entering application code; return reclaimed count or `UINT64_MAX` on rejection/failure
+|===
+
+Calls from U/C mode, nested/active supervisor entry and actions above 3 raise
+illegal-instruction exceptions. Invalid frames, inadmissible context states
+or unavailable supervisor slots raise cause 29 with zero data. The base frame
+must be valid even for forget. A terminal context must be forgotten before
+reusing its frame. Forget does not revoke arenas or scrub all application memory.
+
+[%header,cols="1,2,4"]
+|===
+|Event kind |Meaning |Continuation
+|1 |Quantum/asynchronous escape |Saved context; action 0 resumes at the saved PC
+|2 |Terminal fault |Cannot resume; discard and perform adapter cleanup
+|3 |Service ECALL |Requires option bit 0; cause 11 in this encoding; action 2 supplies a0 and advances PC once
+|4 |Page fault |Requires option bit 1; cause 12, 13 or 15; repair mapping then action 0 retries the same instruction
+|5 |Node pressure |Recycling profile; cause 30, zero data; collect before retrying the allocation instruction
+|===
+
+The event kind is also returned in scalar `rd`. The caller's scalar and
+capability state, privilege and translation/lifetime roots are restored on
+escape. The 5-ms QEMU quantum is platform policy, not a promised ISA timing.
+Physical-supervisor kind zero is not a virtual `RETURN` path: application
+exit uses a service followed by adapter cleanup/forget.
+
+=== Recycling without generations
+
+Recycling is a *trusted stop-and-sweep protocol*, not immediate reuse on
+`REVOKE`. The adapter stops all contexts of N_A and provides every physical
+page that may hold its tags, including entry/reply frames and resident pages
+currently inaccessible through PTEs. Shared tagged mappings are unsupported.
+The page-address array is 16-byte aligned, holds 64-bit physical page bases,
+and currently has 1..262145 entries. Every page must be 4-KiB aligned RAM.
+The whole list is preflighted before tags are changed.
+
+Action 3 clears dead memory tags, demotes dead tagged GPRs in all saved
+contexts with the same root, and pins invalid PCC identities (PCC has no
+separate tag). Only then are unpinned invalid IDs returned to the free list.
+Memory payload bytes need not be erased by this sweep. A stale bit pattern
+without a tag cannot regain capability authority when its ID is reallocated.
+
+QEMU cannot establish that the supplied page inventory is complete. The
+adapter must ensure completeness, exclusion of unregistered tagged aliases
+and namespace quiescence for the entire operation. Table/root reuse and task
+exit require the same accounting. Multi-hart quiescence and tag-preserving
+page migration are not defined by this prototype.
+
+== Transfer faults and exception data
+
+Protected-path `LDC` consumes a tagged source slot whose type is not NONLIN;
+`STC` consumes a tagged non-NONLIN source register. A consuming load needs
+write authority and a writable PTE as well as read authority. All checks,
+including the complete 16-byte span and ordinary RAM backing, precede any
+source consumption or destination publication. A failing operation leaves
+bytes, tags and registers unchanged; a retry transfers once. Read and
+consume must name the same translated physical granule, including when a
+page-table update has not been followed by a translation fence. Legacy
+physical C transfer behavior was not changed by the Q-12 prototype patch.
+
+The capability causes remain 24..30. Capability memory faults carry the
+effective virtual address; virtual PCC faults carry the faulting PC;
+non-memory capability faults and resource exhaustion carry zero. Each raise
+sets explicit fault data and delivery consumes it, preventing reuse of a
+previous trap's address. The direct-U experiment additionally permits
+`medeleg` bits 24..30, but virtual C reports events through `CSRUNV`.
+
+These fault data deliberately differ from the base academic chapter, which
+describes instruction bits for causes 24..29 and withholds cross-world fault
+data. The trusted-OS profile discloses faults and register cursor views to
+the trusted adapter. It does not promise confidentiality against that adapter.
+
+== Encoding and conformance limits
+
+The prototype retains QEMU's existing 128-bit memory representation: the low
+64 bits are the cursor for ordinary capabilities; the upper word contains
+27 bounds bits (0..26), type (27..29), permissions (30..32) and a 31-bit node
+ID (33..63). There are no generation or address-space bits. This is not the
+deployed RTL layout, which allocates 30 node bits and 28 bounds bits.
+
+QEMU still keeps uncompressed bounds alongside physical tags in some paths.
+Although `CSMINT` rejects inexact round trips, general representability of
+`SHRINK`, `SPLIT` and every store/load path remains a separate ISA-freeze
+requirement. A tag-bit-only implementation must never enlarge authority on
+store/load. Existing QEMU application results do not prove that requirement
+or bit-exact RTL conformance.
+
+Before freezing this interface: reconcile both encodings and compressed
+bounds semantics; select permanent instruction/CSR allocations; specify the
+portable context-storage and resource-failure contract; and validate another
+OS adapter. Direct syscalls, full POSIX signal/register editing, fork, tagged
+shared mappings, swap, migration and multi-hart execution require further
+contracts and qualification. This appendix does not imply those features.
+
+== Implementation and review evidence
+
+All paths below are relative to the QEMU reference revision above:
+
+* `target/riscv/insn32.decode`, `insn_trans/trans_capstone.c.inc`, `csr.c`:
+  encodings and privilege checks.
+* `target/riscv/translate.c`, `cpu_helper.c`, `op_helper.c`:
+  virtual translation, PCC checks, fault delivery and consuming transfers.
+* `target/riscv/capstone_table.c`, `cap_rev_table.c`, `cap_rev_tree.c`:
+  mint/retirement, guest records and the common revocation algorithm.
+* `target/riscv/capstone_supervisor.c`: context frame, events and collection.
+* `tests/virtual-capstone-m1/`: recorded 60/60 foundation gate.
+* `tests/virtual-capstone-runtime/`: recorded 33/33 virtual execution gate,
+  including the cached-PCC mutation control; U-access regression 69/69.
+
+The llvm-capstone baseline additionally records runtime, VM, thread, allocator
+and application gates under `capstone/runtime/virtual/` and per-port result
+directories. These are evidence for the recorded revisions, not results of a
+future merge with the shared branches and not a proof of the complete ISA.
